#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119631 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "df6cef5e-c4e3-5f5d-aa68-cfda38cab971" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Fundamental Vector Product of a Parametric Surface"}] :question/id #uuid "175c458d-36cc-5f90-b6ee-976e082c30a2" :question/math-academy-id "e-8943" :question/problem "Find the fundamental vector product of the surface generated by the vector-valued function $\\mathbf{r}(u,v) = \\big\\langle u^2, \\: v^2, \\: u+v \\big\\rangle.$" :question/worked-solution "For a surface with parametrization $\\mathbf r (u,v),$ the fundamental vector product is defined as $\\mathbf{r}'_u \\times \\mathbf{r}'_v.$\n\nWe first compute the tangent vectors to the grid curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{u}^{′} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂u},\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂u},\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂u}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂u}({u}^{2}),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂u}({v}^{2}),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂u}(u+v)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2u,\\,0,\\,1⟩ \\\\ {r}_{v}^{′} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂v},\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂v},\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂v}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂v}({u}^{2}),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂v}({v}^{2}),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂v}(u+v)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,\\,2v,\\,1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the fundamental vector product is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′} & =|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 2u & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 2v & 1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =⟨|\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 2v & 1\\end{aligned}|,\\,-|\\begin{aligned}2u & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}|,\\,|\\begin{aligned}2u & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2v\\end{aligned}|⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-2v,\\,-2u,\\,4uv⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119632 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "9361ad5a-b15c-507b-85d4-fe7ccb4d9fd8" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Surface Area of a Parametric Surface"}] :question/id #uuid "1b4b69f7-fc4f-57c3-bcaf-9df98e4fc5e3" :question/math-academy-id "e-8947" :question/problem "Find the surface area of the parametric surface defined by the equations \n\n$$\nx = u, \\quad \ny = v, \\quad \nz = 1 - u - v\n$$\n\nfor $-1 \\leq u \\leq 1$ and $0 \\leq v \\leq 3.$" :question/worked-solution "For a surface $S$ with parametrization $\\mathbf r (u,v)$ for $(u,v)\\in D,$ the surface area of $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_S \\,\\textrm d S = \\iint\\limits_D  \\left\\|  \\mathbf{r}'_u \\times \\mathbf{r}'_v \\right\\|  \\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nIn vector form, our surface has the equation \n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r}(u,v) = \\left\\langle u, \\: v, \\: 1 - u - v \\right\\rangle.\n$$\n\nWe first compute the tangent vectors to the grid curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{u}^{′} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂u},\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂u},\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂u}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂u}(u),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂u}(v),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂u}(1-u-v)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,\\,0,\\,-1⟩ \\\\ {r}_{v}^{′} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂v},\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂v},\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂v}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂v}(u),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂v}(v),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂v}(1-u-v)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,\\,1,\\,-1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the fundamental vector product is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′} & =|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 1 & 0 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =⟨|\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{aligned}|,\\,-|\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}|,\\,|\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}|⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,\\,1,\\,1⟩,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand its magnitude is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}‖ & =‖⟨1,\\,1,\\,1⟩‖ \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{1}^{2}+{1}^{2}+{1}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{1+1+1} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, let's denote\n\n$$\nD = \\{ (u,v) \\: : \\: -1 \\leq u \\leq 1, \\: 0 \\leq v \\leq 3\\}.\n$$\n\nThen, the surface area is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}\\,\\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}‖{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}‖\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{3}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =\\sqrt{3}{∬}_{D}\\text{ }\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =\\sqrt{3}\\cdot \\text{Area}(D) \\\\  & =\\sqrt{3}\\cdot 2\\cdot 3 \\\\  & =6\\sqrt{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119633 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "2928b0de-fadb-5ffa-b30f-72c0051f0ac6" :knowledge-point/title "Finding an Integral That Represents the Surface Area of a Parametric Surface"}] :question/id #uuid "4b202b4e-d8dc-524c-8cb4-ab0c00eb78cb" :question/math-academy-id "e-8958" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14065243/assets/95e3f2da04e23c9b42ea04cba3ee1d7cc069fef992592f09a70cef367b670f0d.png)\n\nThe integral above gives the surface area of the parametric surface $\\mathbf{r}(u,v) = \\big\\langle u, \\: v, \\: u^3-v \\big\\rangle$ for $0 \\leq u \\leq 3$ and $0 \\leq v \\leq 2.$ What is the missing expression?" :question/worked-solution "For a surface $S$ with parametrization $\\mathbf r (u,v)$ for $(u,v)\\in D,$ the surface area of $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_S \\,\\textrm d S = \\iint\\limits_D  \\left\\|  \\mathbf{r}'_u \\times \\mathbf{r}'_v \\right\\|  \\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWe first compute the tangent vectors to the grid curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{u}^{′} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂u},\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂u},\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂u}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂u}(u),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂u}(v),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂u}({u}^{3}-v)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,\\,0,\\,3{u}^{2}⟩ \\\\ {r}_{v}^{′} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂v},\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂v},\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂v}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂v}(u),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂v}(v),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂v}({u}^{3}-v)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,\\,1,\\,-1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the fundamental vector product is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′} & =|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 1 & 0 & 3{u}^{2} \\\\ 0 & 1 & -1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =⟨|\\begin{aligned}0 & 3{u}^{2} \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{aligned}|,\\,-|\\begin{aligned}1 & 3{u}^{2} \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}|,\\,|\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}|⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-3{u}^{2},\\,1,\\,1⟩,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand its magnitude is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}‖ & =‖⟨-3{u}^{2},\\,1,\\,1⟩‖ \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(3{u}^{2})^{2}+(1)^{2}+(1)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{9{u}^{4}+2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface area is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}\\,\\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}‖{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}‖\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{9{u}^{4}+2}\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119996 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "df6cef5e-c4e3-5f5d-aa68-cfda38cab971" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Fundamental Vector Product of a Parametric Surface"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9283f0f3-aead-5a49-a589-fe4b9f0beadc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨0, 4\\cos^{2} u, v\\cos u⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186116543} {:answer/id #uuid "ca24c4b4-3acf-5e8c-a05e-704e1bafe575" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨0,-4\\cos^{2} u, v\\cos u⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186116544} {:answer/id #uuid "05f0e7f4-6241-5d07-9f45-5f551087aa60" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨8v^{2}\\sin u,-4\\cos^{2} u, 2v\\cos u⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186116545} {:answer/id #uuid "2224c6b7-49bb-586f-8cb8-9afa3d02bd29" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨8v^{2}\\sin u, 4\\cos^{2} u, 2v\\cos u⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186116546} {:answer/id #uuid "7dc2b54e-bb7f-58fc-b1d2-67c4cfc8b260" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨\\cos u, 0,-4v\\sin u⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186116547}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "05f0e7f4-6241-5d07-9f45-5f551087aa60" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨8v^{2}\\sin u,-4\\cos^{2} u, 2v\\cos u⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186116545} :answer-field/id #uuid "33515c78-97bb-5acf-ae0a-409b8db30f44" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186116542}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "cf9a45d8-93e4-5a5c-8ad1-796e86ba6570" :question/math-academy-id "q-105606" :question/problem "Find the fundamental vector product of the surface generated by the vector-valued function $r(u,v)=⟨\\operatorname{sin}⁡u,\\,{v}^{2},\\,4v\\operatorname{cos}⁡u⟩.$" :question/worked-solution "For a surface with parametrization $r(u,v),$ the fundamental vector product is defined as ${r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}.$\n\nWe first compute the tangent vectors to the grid curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{u}^{′} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂u},\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂u},\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂u}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂u}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡u),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂u}({v}^{2}),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂u}(4v\\operatorname{cos}⁡u)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡u,\\,0,\\,-4v\\operatorname{sin}⁡u⟩ \\\\ {r}_{v}^{′} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂v},\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂v},\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂v}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂v}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡u),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂v}({v}^{2}),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂v}(4v\\operatorname{cos}⁡u)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,\\,2v,\\,4\\operatorname{cos}⁡u⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the fundamental vector product is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′} & =|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡u & 0 & -4v\\operatorname{sin}⁡u \\\\ 0 & 2v & 4\\operatorname{cos}⁡u\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =⟨|\\begin{aligned}0 & -4v\\operatorname{sin}⁡u \\\\ 2v & 4\\operatorname{cos}⁡u\\end{aligned}|,\\,-|\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡u & -4v\\operatorname{sin}⁡u \\\\ 0 & 4\\operatorname{cos}⁡u\\end{aligned}|,\\,|\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡u & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2v\\end{aligned}|⟩ \\\\  & =⟨8{v}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡u,\\,-4{cos}^{2}⁡u,\\,2v\\operatorname{cos}⁡u⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119998 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "2928b0de-fadb-5ffa-b30f-72c0051f0ac6" :knowledge-point/title "Finding an Integral That Represents the Surface Area of a Parametric Surface"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c61a9304-5dd7-512b-bc7d-6a18cda5a415" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{u^{2} + 36}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186116555} {:answer/id #uuid "e8de4cc6-8a18-5ed8-85b1-47f1b12f7465" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{3u^{2}\\cos^{2} v + 4u^{2} + 36}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186116556} {:answer/id #uuid "11568d88-6037-507e-aa4a-8026becddba5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6\\sqrt{u^{2}\\sin^{2} v + u^{2} + 1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186116557} {:answer/id #uuid "1acf4c4c-01af-5e86-9f42-9a18f9951f46" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{5}u\\sqrt{\\sin^{2} v + 8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186116558} {:answer/id #uuid "3130095c-2cf7-5c53-bad3-ff45dd01dd6b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6\\sqrt{u^{2} + 1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186116559}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1acf4c4c-01af-5e86-9f42-9a18f9951f46" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{5}u\\sqrt{\\sin^{2} v + 8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186116558} :answer-field/id #uuid "1582649b-a56e-5fb7-a05e-b58b899f607d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186116554}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "d4d337cb-e76c-55f1-8712-82ae25a0b484" :question/math-academy-id "q-105613" :question/problem "$$\n{∬}_{D}\\boxed{}\\,\\text{d}A,\\,D={(u,v)\\,:\\,0\\le u\\le 1,\\,0\\le v\\le \\pi }\n$$\n\nThe integral above gives the surface area of the parametric surface $r(u,v)=⟨2u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v,\\,3u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v,\\,u⟩$ for $0\\le u\\le 1$ and $0\\le v\\le \\pi .$ What is the missing expression?" :question/worked-solution "For a surface $S$ with parametrization $r(u,v)$ for $(u,v)\\in D,$ the surface area of $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}‖{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}‖\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWe first compute the tangent vectors to the grid curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{u}^{′} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂u},\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂u},\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂u}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂u}(2u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂u}(3u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂u}(u)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2\\operatorname{sin}⁡v,\\,3\\operatorname{cos}⁡v,\\,1⟩ \\\\ {r}_{v}^{′} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂v},\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂v},\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂v}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂v}(2u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂v}(3u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂v}(u)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v,\\,-3u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v,\\,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the fundamental vector product is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′} & =|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 2\\operatorname{sin}⁡v & 3\\operatorname{cos}⁡v & 1 \\\\ 2u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v & -3u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v & 0\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =⟨|\\begin{aligned}3\\operatorname{cos}⁡v & 1 \\\\ -3u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v & 0\\end{aligned}|,\\,-|\\begin{aligned}2\\operatorname{sin}⁡v & 1 \\\\ 2u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v & 0\\end{aligned}|,\\,|\\begin{aligned}2\\operatorname{sin}⁡v & 3\\operatorname{cos}⁡v \\\\ 2u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v & -3u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v\\end{aligned}|⟩ \\\\  & =⟨3u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v,\\,2u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v,\\,-6u{sin}^{2}⁡v-6u{cos}^{2}⁡v⟩ \\\\  & =⟨3u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v,\\,2u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v,\\,-6u⟩,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}‖ & =‖⟨3u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v,\\,2u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v,\\,-6u⟩‖ \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(3u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v)^{2}+(2u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v)^{2}+(-6u)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{9{u}^{2}{sin}^{2}⁡v+4{u}^{2}{cos}^{2}⁡v+36{u}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(5{u}^{2}{sin}^{2}⁡v+4{u}^{2}{sin}^{2}⁡v)+4{u}^{2}{cos}^{2}⁡v+36{u}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{5{u}^{2}{sin}^{2}⁡v+4{u}^{2}({sin}^{2}⁡v+{cos}^{2}⁡v)+36{u}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{5{u}^{2}{sin}^{2}⁡v+4{u}^{2}+36{u}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{5{u}^{2}{sin}^{2}⁡v+40{u}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{5{u}^{2}({sin}^{2}⁡v+8)} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{5{u}^{2}}\\sqrt{{sin}^{2}⁡v+8} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{5}u\\sqrt{{sin}^{2}⁡v+8}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that $\\sqrt{{u}^{2}}=u$ since $u\\ge 0$ on $D.$\n\nTherefore, the surface area is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}\\,\\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}‖{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}‖\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{5}u\\sqrt{{sin}^{2}⁡v+8}\\,\\text{d}A\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120003 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "9361ad5a-b15c-507b-85d4-fe7ccb4d9fd8" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Surface Area of a Parametric Surface"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8dcb7b39-06db-5eb2-8bcb-3def15a34421" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{30}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186116585} {:answer/id #uuid "ad96ef07-86de-5324-912c-808ca6a92b73" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186116586} {:answer/id #uuid "cc274ea7-401a-5676-80dc-6ee01923d282" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{70}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186116587} {:answer/id #uuid "de6feef4-1ae0-5da7-befe-66de7c727461" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "20" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186116588} {:answer/id #uuid "3ae5a2ba-c746-5cbc-aec9-fdb98e82e80b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{35}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186116589}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "cc274ea7-401a-5676-80dc-6ee01923d282" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{70}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186116587} :answer-field/id #uuid "b1cd35e9-52eb-5e41-81af-424b36fb5ff2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186116584}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "04aeca54-3b07-58c4-888a-6b39f758095a" :question/math-academy-id "q-105920" :question/problem "Find the surface area of the parametric surface defined by the equations \n\n$$\nx=2u-v,\\,y=3u+2,\\,z=u+2v\n$$\n\nfor $0\\le u\\le 1$ and $-1\\le v\\le 1.$" :question/worked-solution "For a surface $S$ with parametrization $r(u,v)$ for $(u,v)\\in D,$ the surface area of $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}‖{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}‖\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nIn vector form, our surface has the equation \n\n$$\nr(u,v)=⟨2u-v,\\,3u+2,\\,u+2v⟩.\n$$\n\nWe first compute the tangent vectors to the grid curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{u}^{′} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂u},\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂u},\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂u}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂u}(2u-v),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂u}(3u+2),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂u}(u+2v)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2,\\,3,\\,1⟩ \\\\ {r}_{v}^{′} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂v},\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂v},\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂v}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂v}(2u-v),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂v}(3u+2),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂v}(u+2v)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-1,\\,0,\\,2⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the fundamental vector product is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′} & =|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 2 & 3 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =⟨|\\begin{aligned}3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}|,\\,-|\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{aligned}|,\\,|\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{aligned}|⟩ \\\\  & =⟨6,\\,-5,\\,3⟩,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}‖ & =‖⟨6,\\,-5,\\,3⟩‖ \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{6}^{2}+(-5)^{2}+{3}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{36+25+9} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{70}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, let's denote\n\n$$\nD={(u,v)\\,:\\,0\\le u\\le 1,\\,-1\\le v\\le 1}.\n$$\n\nThen, the surface area is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}\\,\\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}‖{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}‖\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{70}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =\\sqrt{70}{∬}_{D}\\text{ }\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =\\sqrt{70}\\cdot \\text{Area}(D) \\\\  & =\\sqrt{70}\\cdot 1\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{70}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120004 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "9361ad5a-b15c-507b-85d4-fe7ccb4d9fd8" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Surface Area of a Parametric Surface"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8d65b53a-dfd4-59e8-9d95-75179941fba0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi \\sqrt{38}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349828} {:answer/id #uuid "7a71d87f-5750-57f9-900f-376dda192119" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi \\sqrt{38}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349829} {:answer/id #uuid "11adbc35-b143-548e-982f-906693114ddb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi \\sqrt{19}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349830} {:answer/id #uuid "439c6587-30ba-5202-a17b-6113399d0abc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi \\sqrt{57}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349831} {:answer/id #uuid "1255d1a3-3102-5935-9f83-8d44436f07ee" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\pi \\sqrt{19}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349832}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1255d1a3-3102-5935-9f83-8d44436f07ee" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\pi \\sqrt{19}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349832} :answer-field/id #uuid "9d13fda1-29d0-52e7-a58f-c8f26fc87d08" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349827}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "fae50a6d-3a64-5292-bf93-f5341ecb7d4d" :question/math-academy-id "q-105922" :question/problem "Find the surface area of the part of the parametric surface $r(u,v)=⟨-u+v,\\,u-2v,\\,3v⟩$ that is subject to the constraint ${u}^{2}+{v}^{2}\\le 4.$" :question/worked-solution "For a surface $S$ with parametrization $r(u,v)$ for $(u,v)\\in D,$ the surface area of $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}‖{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}‖\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWe first compute the tangent vectors to the grid curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{u}^{′} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂u},\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂u},\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂u}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂u}(-u+v),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂u}(u-2v),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂u}(3v)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-1,\\,1,\\,0⟩ \\\\ {r}_{v}^{′} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂v},\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂v},\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂v}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂v}(-u+v),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂v}(u-2v),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂v}(3v)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,\\,-2,\\,3⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the fundamental vector product is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′} & =|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ -1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -2 & 3\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =⟨|\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 3\\end{aligned}|,\\,-|\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{aligned}|,\\,|\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -2\\end{aligned}|⟩ \\\\  & =⟨3,\\,3,\\,1⟩,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}‖ & =‖⟨3,\\,3,\\,1⟩‖ \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{3}^{2}+{3}^{2}+{1}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{9+9+1} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{19}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, notice that the region ${u}^{2}+{v}^{2}\\le 4$ corresponds to the disk\n\n$$\nD={(u,v)\\,:\\,{u}^{2}+{v}^{2}\\le 4},\n$$\n\nof radius $2.$\n\nTherefore, the surface area is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}\\,\\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}‖{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}‖\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{19}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =\\sqrt{19}{∬}_{D}\\text{d}A \\\\  & =\\sqrt{19}\\cdot \\text{Area}(D) \\\\  & =\\sqrt{19}\\cdot (\\pi \\cdot {2}^{2}) \\\\  & =4\\pi \\sqrt{19}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186349833 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "9361ad5a-b15c-507b-85d4-fe7ccb4d9fd8" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Surface Area of a Parametric Surface"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "28097084-cd2f-5d11-a905-838c51b4f38a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8\\sqrt{11}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349835} {:answer/id #uuid "90678774-68c4-545b-9483-6a1461f1dafb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\sqrt{22}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349836} {:answer/id #uuid "655d4d22-905b-5f18-b5e3-077013d16d16" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349837} {:answer/id #uuid "94d56868-8c17-5a5f-ade7-949c66a90a6d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\sqrt{14}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349838} {:answer/id #uuid "74eaa459-d00c-5559-a1ab-b97890382db6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5\\sqrt{10}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349839}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "90678774-68c4-545b-9483-6a1461f1dafb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\sqrt{22}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349836} :answer-field/id #uuid "51171050-cc34-549b-bd54-7fa6031ebbb4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349834}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "1f5df557-9029-5b60-aa1b-2432aa8f8375" :question/math-academy-id "q-109710" :question/problem "Find the surface area of the parametric surface defined by the equations \n\n$$\nx=u+v,\\,y=2-3u,\\,z=1+u-v\n$$\n\nfor $0\\le u\\le 2$ and $-1\\le v\\le 1.$" :question/worked-solution "For a surface $S$ with parametrization $r(u,v)$ for $(u,v)\\in D,$ the surface area of $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}‖{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}‖\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nIn vector form, our surface has the equation \n\n$$\nr(u,v)=⟨u+v,\\,2-3u,\\,1+u-v⟩.\n$$\n\nWe first compute the tangent vectors to the grid curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{u}^{′} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂u},\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂u},\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂u}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂u}(u+v),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂u}(2-3u),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂u}(1+u-v)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,\\,-3,\\,1⟩ \\\\ {r}_{v}^{′} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂v},\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂v},\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂v}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂v}(u+v),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂v}(2-3u),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂v}(1+u-v)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,\\,0,\\,-1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the fundamental vector product is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′} & =|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 1 & -3 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0 & -1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =⟨|\\begin{aligned}-3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}|,\\,-|\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{aligned}|,\\,|\\begin{aligned}1 & -3 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}|⟩ \\\\  & =⟨3,\\,2,\\,3⟩,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}‖ & =‖⟨3,\\,2,\\,3⟩‖ \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{3}^{2}+{2}^{2}+{3}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{9+4+9} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{22}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, let's denote\n\n$$\nD={(u,v)\\,:\\,0\\le u\\le 2,\\,-1\\le v\\le 1}.\n$$\n\nThen, the surface area is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}\\,\\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}‖{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}‖\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{22}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =\\sqrt{22}{∬}_{D}\\text{d}A \\\\  & =\\sqrt{22}\\cdot \\text{Area}(D) \\\\  & =\\sqrt{22}\\cdot 2\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =4\\sqrt{22}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186349840 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "9361ad5a-b15c-507b-85d4-fe7ccb4d9fd8" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Surface Area of a Parametric Surface"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5366922f-ef1e-5177-8d83-df524ad3985b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349842} {:answer/id #uuid "67066575-1eca-5476-b758-b17436b801aa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349843} {:answer/id #uuid "45fa93cc-ac26-58dd-99f9-51d2ce21a8eb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349844} {:answer/id #uuid "545e14ca-e715-5433-ba50-b5b99e53201a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349845} {:answer/id #uuid "467c0f4f-4fae-559a-a195-2df06a924aff" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{10}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349846}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "45fa93cc-ac26-58dd-99f9-51d2ce21a8eb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349844} :answer-field/id #uuid "b0af2b1e-d660-583b-a284-f0cea367efca" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349841}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "fde6b4fa-f661-5e9b-8ea9-671ddd4a683e" :question/math-academy-id "q-109761" :question/problem "Find the surface area of the parametric surface defined by the equations \n\n$$\nx={u}^{2},\\,y=uv,\\,z=\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2}\n$$\n\nfor $0\\le u\\le 1$ and $1\\le v\\le 2.$" :question/worked-solution "For a surface $S$ with parametrization $r(u,v)$ for $(u,v)\\in D,$ the surface area of $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}‖{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}‖\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nIn vector form, our surface has the equation \n\n$$\nr(u,v)=⟨{u}^{2},\\,uv,\\,\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2}⟩.\n$$\n\nWe first compute the tangent vectors to the grid curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{u}^{′} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂u},\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂u},\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂u}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂u}({u}^{2}),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂u}(uv),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂u}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2u,\\,v,\\,0⟩ \\\\ {r}_{v}^{′} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂v},\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂v},\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂v}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂v}({u}^{2}),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂v}(uv),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂v}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,\\,u,\\,v⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the fundamental vector product is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′} & =|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 2u & v & 0 \\\\ 0 & u & v\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =⟨|\\begin{aligned}v & 0 \\\\ u & v\\end{aligned}|,\\,-|\\begin{aligned}2u & 0 \\\\ 0 & v\\end{aligned}|,\\,|\\begin{aligned}2u & v \\\\ 0 & u\\end{aligned}|⟩ \\\\  & =⟨{v}^{2},\\,-2uv,\\,2{u}^{2}⟩,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}‖ & =‖⟨{v}^{2},\\,-2uv,\\,2{u}^{2}⟩‖ \\\\  & =\\sqrt{({v}^{2})^{2}+(-2uv)^{2}+(2{u}^{2})^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{v}^{4}+4{u}^{2}{v}^{2}+4{u}^{4}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{({v}^{2}+2{u}^{2})^{2}} \\\\  & ={v}^{2}+2{u}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, let's denote\n\n$$\nD={(u,v)\\,:\\,0\\le u\\le 1,\\,1\\le v\\le 2}.\n$$\n\nThen, the surface area is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}\\,\\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}‖{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}‖\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}({v}^{2}+2{u}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}({v}^{2}+2{u}^{2})\\,\\text{d}u\\,\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}{[u{v}^{2}+\\frac{2}{3}{u}^{3}]}_{0}^{1}\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}({v}^{2}+\\frac{2}{3})\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={[\\frac{1}{3}{v}^{3}+\\frac{2}{3}v]}_{1}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}[{2}^{3}+2(2)-({1}^{3}+2(1))] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}(9) \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186349847 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "df6cef5e-c4e3-5f5d-aa68-cfda38cab971" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Fundamental Vector Product of a Parametric Surface"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e0b288a4-9541-53be-ba2b-50ec4df030e7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨-2u,\\,2v,\\,1⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349849} {:answer/id #uuid "208b4f8c-b059-5dbd-b13b-0e75ca148590" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨u,\\,-2v,\\,u+v⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349850} {:answer/id #uuid "f4cdf01e-104d-5888-97a1-04368c6cedfe" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨-2u,\\,v+u,\\,2v⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349851} {:answer/id #uuid "ed33ccc0-6cd7-5840-b237-0ef0244dc636" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨u,\\,u-2v,\\,v⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349852} {:answer/id #uuid "bf32057c-c177-5bd2-b21e-1958c346343e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨-2u,\\,2v,\\,2u-2v⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349853}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e0b288a4-9541-53be-ba2b-50ec4df030e7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨-2u,\\,2v,\\,1⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349849} :answer-field/id #uuid "e393327e-723f-5656-b501-a024f8872ffb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349848}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "40d162ad-d136-5925-ad85-94494d178152" :question/math-academy-id "q-109904" :question/problem "Find the fundamental vector product of the surface defined by the equations \n\n$$\nx=u,\\,y=v,\\,z={u}^{2}-{v}^{2}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "For a surface with parametrization $r(u,v),$ the fundamental vector product is defined as ${r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}.$\n\nIn vector form, our surface has the equation \n\n$$\nr(u,v)=⟨u,\\,v,\\,{u}^{2}-{v}^{2}⟩.\n$$\n\nWe first compute the tangent vectors to the grid curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{u}^{′} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂u},\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂u},\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂u}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂u}(u),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂u}(v),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂u}({u}^{2}-{v}^{2})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,\\,0,\\,2u⟩ \\\\ {r}_{v}^{′} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂v},\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂v},\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂v}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂v}(u),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂v}(v),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂v}({u}^{2}-{v}^{2})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,\\,1,\\,-2v⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the fundamental vector product is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′} & =|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 1 & 0 & 2u \\\\ 0 & 1 & -2v\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =⟨|\\begin{aligned}0 & 2u \\\\ 1 & -2v\\end{aligned}|,\\,-|\\begin{aligned}1 & 2u \\\\ 0 & -2v\\end{aligned}|,\\,|\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}|⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-2u,\\,2v,\\,1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186349854 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "df6cef5e-c4e3-5f5d-aa68-cfda38cab971" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Fundamental Vector Product of a Parametric Surface"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "581c8d6d-3ca1-5253-bf1e-910f17889f7e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨-{e}^{v},\\,-1,\\,1⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349856} {:answer/id #uuid "e98dd99a-b668-576a-b864-69b3e1b66a29" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨v,\\,-{e}^{v},\\,u⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349857} {:answer/id #uuid "e88955f4-c198-5298-9d9c-8d8e2b6e5fcc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨-1,\\,{e}^{v},\\,1⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349858} {:answer/id #uuid "6a324eff-551b-5e6f-bda0-b77fafca81de" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨-u,\\,{e}^{v},\\,1⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349859} {:answer/id #uuid "8849d295-94fb-5771-bea7-d791292634b2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨-v,\\,-{e}^{v},\\,{e}^{v}⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349860}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e88955f4-c198-5298-9d9c-8d8e2b6e5fcc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨-1,\\,{e}^{v},\\,1⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349858} :answer-field/id #uuid "0969b040-2568-547a-a192-0a1247dea531" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349855}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "bdb4f741-3811-51f6-9c76-a70af15f67af" :question/math-academy-id "q-109909" :question/problem "Find the fundamental vector product of the surface defined by the equations \n\n$$\nx=u,\\,y=v,\\,z=u-{e}^{v}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "For a surface with parametrization $r(u,v),$ the fundamental vector product is defined as ${r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}.$\n\nIn vector form, our surface has the equation \n\n$$\nr(u,v)=⟨u,\\,v,\\,u-{e}^{v}⟩.\n$$\n\nWe first compute the tangent vectors to the grid curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{u}^{′} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂u},\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂u},\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂u}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂u}(u),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂u}(v),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂u}(u-{e}^{v})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,\\,0,\\,1⟩ \\\\ {r}_{v}^{′} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂v},\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂v},\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂v}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂v}(u),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂v}(v),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂v}(u-{e}^{v})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,\\,1,\\,-{e}^{v}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the fundamental vector product is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′} & =|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 1 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -{e}^{v}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =⟨|\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -{e}^{v}\\end{aligned}|,\\,-|\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -{e}^{v}\\end{aligned}|,\\,|\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}|⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-1,\\,{e}^{v},\\,1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186349861 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "df6cef5e-c4e3-5f5d-aa68-cfda38cab971" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Fundamental Vector Product of a Parametric Surface"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2fc04779-ea73-5a07-9c2f-4747f1d4c4e9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨-6{v}^{2}+u,\\,-2u+v,\\,6u{v}^{2}⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349863} {:answer/id #uuid "616d2781-3827-5fdb-9283-091a9f89142c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨-6{v}^{2},\\,-2u,\\,6u{v}^{2}⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349864} {:answer/id #uuid "e097e739-8945-5719-b674-22b11631b866" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨{v}^{2}-u,\\,2v,\\,u{v}^{2}⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349865} {:answer/id #uuid "92ba9708-68e3-5669-853c-90cb45b088ce" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨u-v,\\,2u,\\,{v}^{2}-u⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349866} {:answer/id #uuid "9480fa4a-8952-522a-9250-644ec1b8fa10" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨v,\\,-2u,\\,-3u{v}^{2}⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349867}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "616d2781-3827-5fdb-9283-091a9f89142c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨-6{v}^{2},\\,-2u,\\,6u{v}^{2}⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349864} :answer-field/id #uuid "84d27442-6a52-560d-acea-fb1101a6008e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349862}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "6c6fc83a-716f-5522-988b-a827a24228ea" :question/math-academy-id "q-109911" :question/problem "Find the fundamental vector product of the surface defined by the equations \n\n$$\nx={u}^{2}+1,\\,y={v}^{3}+1,\\,z=2u+v.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "For a surface with parametrization $r(u,v),$ the fundamental vector product is defined as ${r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}.$\n\nIn vector form, our surface has the equation \n\n$$\nr(u,v)=⟨{u}^{2}+1,\\,{v}^{3}+1,\\,2u+v⟩.\n$$\n\nWe first compute the tangent vectors to the grid curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{u}^{′} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂u},\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂u},\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂u}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂u}({u}^{2}+1),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂u}({v}^{3}+1),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂u}(2u+v)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2u,\\,0,\\,2⟩ \\\\ {r}_{v}^{′} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂v},\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂v},\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂v}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂v}({u}^{2}),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂v}({v}^{3}+1),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂v}(2u+v)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,\\,3{v}^{2},\\,1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the fundamental vector product is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′} & =|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 2u & 0 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 3{v}^{2} & 1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =⟨|\\begin{aligned}0 & 2 \\\\ 3{v}^{2} & 1\\end{aligned}|,\\,-|\\begin{aligned}2u & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}|,\\,|\\begin{aligned}2u & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3{v}^{2}\\end{aligned}|⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-6{v}^{2},\\,-2u,\\,6u{v}^{2}⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186349868 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "df6cef5e-c4e3-5f5d-aa68-cfda38cab971" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Fundamental Vector Product of a Parametric Surface"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "05a2d210-0eae-5120-964c-548312c60db8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨\\operatorname{sin}⁡u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v-uv\\operatorname{cos}⁡u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v,\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡u,\\,-u\\operatorname{cos}⁡u⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349870} {:answer/id #uuid "90605b8f-2c68-5b0a-b0f2-c2fc52b8b6b9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨uv\\operatorname{cos}⁡u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v,\\,-\\operatorname{cos}⁡v,\\,u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349871} {:answer/id #uuid "be51671e-f6c4-577a-b17e-abb7b65d435b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨\\operatorname{sin}⁡u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v,\\,-\\operatorname{cos}⁡v,\\,v\\operatorname{cos}⁡u⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349872} {:answer/id #uuid "70f3d8ae-d688-5813-be98-1433ef35d848" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨uv\\operatorname{cos}⁡u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v,\\,-v\\operatorname{cos}⁡u,\\,u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349873} {:answer/id #uuid "8f968ba7-7e7b-5b64-a5f7-0325a6c60abb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v+uv\\operatorname{sin}⁡u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v,\\,-\\operatorname{cos}⁡u,\\,u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349874}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8f968ba7-7e7b-5b64-a5f7-0325a6c60abb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v+uv\\operatorname{sin}⁡u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v,\\,-\\operatorname{cos}⁡u,\\,u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349874} :answer-field/id #uuid "fe50237d-9e81-5c7c-8d50-adad84ff5502" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349869}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "9f52e90e-7be3-5f9c-9e1c-12885e700253" :question/math-academy-id "q-109912" :question/problem "Find the fundamental vector product of the surface generated by the vector-valued function $r(u,v)=⟨u,\\,u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v,\\,v\\operatorname{cos}⁡u⟩.$" :question/worked-solution "For a surface with parametrization $r(u,v),$ the fundamental vector product is defined as ${r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}.$\n\nWe first compute the tangent vectors to the grid curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{u}^{′} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂u},\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂u},\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂u}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂u}(u),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂u}(u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂u}(v\\operatorname{cos}⁡u)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡v,\\,-v\\operatorname{sin}⁡u⟩ \\\\ {r}_{v}^{′} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂v},\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂v},\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂v}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂v}(u),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂v}(u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂v}(v\\operatorname{cos}⁡u)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,\\,u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v,\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡u⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the fundamental vector product is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′} & =|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 1 & \\operatorname{sin}⁡v & -v\\operatorname{sin}⁡u \\\\ 0 & u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v & \\operatorname{cos}⁡u\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =⟨|\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡v & -v\\operatorname{sin}⁡u \\\\ u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v & \\operatorname{cos}⁡u\\end{aligned}|,\\,-|\\begin{aligned}1 & -v\\operatorname{sin}⁡u \\\\ 0 & \\operatorname{cos}⁡u\\end{aligned}|,\\,|\\begin{aligned}1 & \\operatorname{sin}⁡v \\\\ 0 & u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v\\end{aligned}|⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v+uv\\operatorname{sin}⁡u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v,\\,-\\operatorname{cos}⁡u,\\,u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120016 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "2928b0de-fadb-5ffa-b30f-72c0051f0ac6" :knowledge-point/title "Finding an Integral That Represents the Surface Area of a Parametric Surface"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "bbcb1378-f815-5d7e-8e39-2a358ace881e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "u\\sqrt{1 + 4v}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186116662} {:answer/id #uuid "e3f18a53-93c2-53a6-9272-f10c85d99e97" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{u^{2} + v^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186116663} {:answer/id #uuid "fcfe140b-fbcf-5062-b6eb-31a6494b119d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "v^{2}\\sqrt{1 + 4u^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186116664} {:answer/id #uuid "1bcb8427-65ea-5ad2-b4c4-f13dfc918eb9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{v^{2} - u^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186116665} {:answer/id #uuid "7b8cddd5-3814-5c4e-a7ef-8fdb5218676a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2v\\sqrt{1 + 8u^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186116666}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7b8cddd5-3814-5c4e-a7ef-8fdb5218676a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2v\\sqrt{1 + 8u^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186116666} :answer-field/id #uuid "9cdad585-8c40-5273-9afc-8b7048317533" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186116661}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "a126827b-bc56-516e-a030-80e53d51bfcf" :question/math-academy-id "q-109941" :question/problem "$$\n{∬}_{D}\\boxed{}\\,\\text{d}A,\\,D={(u,v)\\,:\\,0\\le u\\le 2,\\,0\\le v\\le 2}\n$$\n\nThe integral above gives the surface area of the parametric surface $r(u,v)=⟨{u}^{2},\\,u-{v}^{2},\\,{v}^{2}⟩$ for $0\\le u\\le 2$ and $0\\le v\\le 2.$ What is the missing expression?" :question/worked-solution "For a surface $S$ with parametrization $r(u,v)$ for $(u,v)\\in D,$ the surface area of $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}‖{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}‖\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWe first compute the tangent vectors to the grid curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{u}^{′} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂u},\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂u},\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂u}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂u}({u}^{2}),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂u}(u-{v}^{2}),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂u}({v}^{2})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2u,\\,1,\\,0⟩ \\\\ {r}_{v}^{′} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂v},\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂v},\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂v}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂v}({u}^{2}),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂v}(u-{v}^{2}),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂v}({v}^{2})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,\\,-2v,\\,2v⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the fundamental vector product is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′} & =|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 2u & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2v & 2v\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =⟨|\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ -2v & 2v\\end{aligned}|,\\,-|\\begin{aligned}2u & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2v\\end{aligned}|,\\,|\\begin{aligned}2u & 1 \\\\ 0 & -2v\\end{aligned}|⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2v,\\,-4uv,\\,-4uv⟩,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}‖ & =‖⟨2v,\\,-4uv,\\,-4uv⟩‖ \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(2v)^{2}+(-4uv)^{2}+(-4uv)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4{v}^{2}+16{u}^{2}{v}^{2}+16{u}^{2}{v}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4{v}^{2}+32{u}^{2}{v}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4{v}^{2}(1+8{u}^{2})} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4{v}^{2}}\\sqrt{1+8{u}^{2}} \\\\  & =2v\\sqrt{1+8{u}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that $\\sqrt{{v}^{2}}=v$ since $v\\ge 0$ on $D.$\n\nTherefore, the surface area is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}\\,\\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}‖{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}‖\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}2v\\sqrt{1+8{u}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186349875 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "2928b0de-fadb-5ffa-b30f-72c0051f0ac6" :knowledge-point/title "Finding an Integral That Represents the Surface Area of a Parametric Surface"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "15b3da73-1805-5927-8615-273fae3abd78" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{10}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349877} {:answer/id #uuid "10798e5a-4688-549f-9b18-79dc2398de47" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349878} {:answer/id #uuid "a9d5b90e-3eb4-5503-b181-8bd5e4d334a6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "u\\sqrt{{v}^{2}+1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349879} {:answer/id #uuid "d18f7c9c-5b24-5d63-b06c-d575e6854c7d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{u+v}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349880} {:answer/id #uuid "ee85c82e-3375-5628-baad-56f669b1044e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349881}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ee85c82e-3375-5628-baad-56f669b1044e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349881} :answer-field/id #uuid "05b54108-a654-5d8c-8c13-657eeb0b8940" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349876}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "8259cc12-8425-5d9e-923a-3106edaae5d3" :question/math-academy-id "q-109943" :question/problem "$$\n{∬}_{D}\\boxed{}\\,\\text{d}A,\\,D={(u,v)\\,:\\,0\\le u\\le 1,\\,0\\le v\\le 2}\n$$\n\nThe integral above gives the surface area of the parametric surface $r(u,v)=⟨u+v,\\,3-v,\\,1+4u+5v⟩$ for $0\\le u\\le 1$ and $0\\le v\\le 2.$ What is the missing expression?" :question/worked-solution "For a surface $S$ with parametrization $r(u,v)$ for $(u,v)\\in D,$ the surface area of $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}‖{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}‖\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWe first compute the tangent vectors to the grid curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{u}^{′} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂u},\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂u},\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂u}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂u}(u+v),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂u}(3-v),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂u}(1+4u+5v)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,\\,0,\\,4⟩ \\\\ {r}_{v}^{′} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂v},\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂v},\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂v}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂v}(u+v),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂v}(3-v),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂v}(1+4u+5v)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,\\,-1,\\,5⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the fundamental vector product is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′} & =|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 1 & 0 & 4 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 5\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =⟨|\\begin{aligned}0 & 4 \\\\ -1 & 5\\end{aligned}|,\\,-|\\begin{aligned}1 & 4 \\\\ 1 & 5\\end{aligned}|,\\,|\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{aligned}|⟩ \\\\  & =⟨4,\\,-1,\\,-1⟩,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}‖ & =‖⟨4,\\,-1,\\,-1⟩‖ \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(4)^{2}+(-1)^{2}+(-1)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{16+1+1} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{18} \\\\  & =3\\sqrt{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface area is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}\\,\\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}‖{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}‖\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}3\\sqrt{2}\\,\\text{d}A\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186349882 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "2928b0de-fadb-5ffa-b30f-72c0051f0ac6" :knowledge-point/title "Finding an Integral That Represents the Surface Area of a Parametric Surface"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d41bc9b6-8ed7-5208-9037-0e558f9d65b6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{{e}^{u}\\operatorname{cos}⁡v\\operatorname{sin}⁡v}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349884} {:answer/id #uuid "4c991233-c89a-5369-b15d-2477a5aadbb1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{u}\\sqrt{{e}^{2u}+1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349885} {:answer/id #uuid "8ab39181-7084-517d-b85b-b76eb0fe1301" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{u}\\operatorname{cos}⁡v\\operatorname{sin}⁡v" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349886} {:answer/id #uuid "81d307ee-6165-5894-a0b6-9b509b5377f8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{u}\\sqrt{u+1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349887} {:answer/id #uuid "6e041be8-0309-5bdf-9a4b-36d35cd379d3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{2u}\\sqrt{\\operatorname{cos}⁡v+\\operatorname{sin}⁡v}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349888}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4c991233-c89a-5369-b15d-2477a5aadbb1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{u}\\sqrt{{e}^{2u}+1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349885} :answer-field/id #uuid "973d7a4c-1ac1-5179-af41-3090caec0b90" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349883}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "a62dc909-eec2-53d1-902b-79e1964b1d9f" :question/math-academy-id "q-109948" :question/problem "$$\n{∬}_{D}\\boxed{}\\,\\text{d}A,\\,D={(u,v)\\,:\\,0\\le u\\le 4,\\,0\\le v\\le \\pi }\n$$\n\nThe integral above gives the surface area of the parametric surface $r(u,v)=⟨u,\\,{e}^{u}\\operatorname{sin}⁡v,\\,{e}^{u}\\operatorname{cos}⁡v⟩$ for $0\\le u\\le 4$ and $0\\le v\\le \\pi .$ What is the missing expression?" :question/worked-solution "For a surface $S$ with parametrization $r(u,v)$ for $(u,v)\\in D,$ the surface area of $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}‖{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}‖\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWe first compute the tangent vectors to the grid curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{u}^{′} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂u},\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂u},\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂u}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂u}(u),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂u}({e}^{u}\\operatorname{sin}⁡v),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂u}({e}^{u}\\operatorname{cos}⁡v)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,\\,{e}^{u}\\operatorname{sin}⁡v,\\,{e}^{u}\\operatorname{cos}⁡v⟩ \\\\ {r}_{v}^{′} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂v},\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂v},\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂v}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂v}(u),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂v}({e}^{u}\\operatorname{sin}⁡v),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂v}({e}^{u}\\operatorname{cos}⁡v)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,\\,{e}^{u}\\operatorname{cos}⁡v,\\,-{e}^{u}\\operatorname{sin}⁡v⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the fundamental vector product is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′} & =|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 1 & {e}^{u}\\operatorname{sin}⁡v & {e}^{u}\\operatorname{cos}⁡v \\\\ 0 & {e}^{u}\\operatorname{cos}⁡v & -{e}^{u}\\operatorname{sin}⁡v\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =⟨|\\begin{aligned}{e}^{u}\\operatorname{sin}⁡v & {e}^{u}\\operatorname{cos}⁡v \\\\ {e}^{u}\\operatorname{cos}⁡v & -{e}^{u}\\operatorname{sin}⁡v\\end{aligned}|,\\,-|\\begin{aligned}1 & {e}^{u}\\operatorname{cos}⁡v \\\\ 0 & -{e}^{u}\\operatorname{sin}⁡v\\end{aligned}|,\\,|\\begin{aligned}1 & {e}^{u}\\operatorname{sin}⁡v \\\\ 0 & {e}^{u}\\operatorname{cos}⁡v\\end{aligned}|⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-{e}^{2u}({sin}^{2}⁡v+{cos}^{2}⁡v),\\,{e}^{u}\\operatorname{sin}⁡v,\\,{e}^{u}\\operatorname{cos}⁡v⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-{e}^{2u},\\,{e}^{u}\\operatorname{sin}⁡v,\\,{e}^{u}\\operatorname{cos}⁡v⟩,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}‖ & =‖⟨-{e}^{2u},\\,{e}^{u}\\operatorname{sin}⁡v,\\,{e}^{u}\\operatorname{cos}⁡v⟩‖ \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-{e}^{2u})^{2}+({e}^{u}\\operatorname{sin}⁡v)^{2}+({e}^{u}\\operatorname{cos}⁡v)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{e}^{4u}+{e}^{2u}{sin}^{2}⁡v+{e}^{2u}{cos}^{2}⁡v} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{e}^{4u}+{e}^{2u}({sin}^{2}⁡v+{cos}^{2}⁡v)} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{e}^{4u}+{e}^{2u}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{e}^{2u}({e}^{2u}+1)} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{e}^{2u}}\\sqrt{{e}^{2u}+1} \\\\  & ={e}^{u}\\sqrt{{e}^{2u}+1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface area is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}\\,\\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}‖{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}‖\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}{e}^{u}\\sqrt{{e}^{2u}+1}\\,\\text{d}A\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186349889 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "2928b0de-fadb-5ffa-b30f-72c0051f0ac6" :knowledge-point/title "Finding an Integral That Represents the Surface Area of a Parametric Surface"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ba3c2e6c-275f-5dfe-9fee-2acc8a62a398" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{{u}^{2}+{v}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349891} {:answer/id #uuid "4759a7de-1030-5d87-8262-d334c840bf73" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{{u}^{4}+{v}^{4}+4{u}^{2}{v}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349892} {:answer/id #uuid "563ccb26-35c5-5eca-9dc8-f1db52f353a0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{u}^{2}v" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349893} {:answer/id #uuid "e4825d64-b7ff-5752-a16c-bd28bf7ff619" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "u\\sqrt{{u}^{2}+1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349894} {:answer/id #uuid "6d24f711-344a-5ac6-adb8-309df512ae5d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2u\\sqrt{{v}^{2}+uv}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349895}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4759a7de-1030-5d87-8262-d334c840bf73" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{{u}^{4}+{v}^{4}+4{u}^{2}{v}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349892} :answer-field/id #uuid "01a39306-d217-54ae-9bab-2089fb4653de" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349890}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "0aca92da-c648-5c8b-ae46-3b81f8b99c27" :question/math-academy-id "q-109951" :question/problem "$$\n{∬}_{D}\\boxed{}\\,\\text{d}A,\\,D={(u,v)\\,:\\,0\\le u\\le 2,\\,0\\le v\\le 4}\n$$\n\nThe integral above gives the surface area of the parametric surface $r(u,v)=⟨{u}^{2},\\,{v}^{2},\\,uv⟩$ for $0\\le u\\le 2$ and $0\\le v\\le 4.$ What is the missing expression?" :question/worked-solution "For a surface $S$ with parametrization $r(u,v)$ for $(u,v)\\in D,$ the surface area of $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}‖{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}‖\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWe first compute the tangent vectors to the grid curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{u}^{′} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂u},\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂u},\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂u}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂u}({u}^{2}),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂u}({v}^{2}),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂u}(uv)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2u,\\,0,\\,v⟩ \\\\ {r}_{v}^{′} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂v},\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂v},\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂v}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂v}({u}^{2}),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂v}({v}^{2}),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂v}(uv)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,\\,2v,\\,u⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the fundamental vector product is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′} & =|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 2u & 0 & v \\\\ 0 & 2v & u\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =⟨|\\begin{aligned}0 & v \\\\ 2v & u\\end{aligned}|,\\,-|\\begin{aligned}2u & v \\\\ 0 & u\\end{aligned}|,\\,|\\begin{aligned}2u & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2v\\end{aligned}|⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-2{v}^{2},\\,-2{u}^{2},\\,4uv⟩,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}‖ & =‖⟨-2{v}^{2},\\,-2{u}^{2},\\,4uv⟩‖ \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-2{v}^{2})^{2}+(-2{u}^{2})^{2}+(4uv)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4{v}^{4}+4{u}^{4}+16{u}^{2}{v}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4({u}^{4}+{v}^{4}+4{u}^{2}{v}^{2})} \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{{u}^{4}+{v}^{4}+4{u}^{2}{v}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface area is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}\\,\\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}‖{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}‖\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}2\\sqrt{{u}^{4}+{v}^{4}+4{u}^{2}{v}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186349896 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "9361ad5a-b15c-507b-85d4-fe7ccb4d9fd8" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Surface Area of a Parametric Surface"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8c8fce93-9300-5cc2-913e-8ba49f7ac6c4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\pi \\sqrt{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349898} {:answer/id #uuid "4c8d69c2-2d51-5fea-89ac-da831b4db913" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi \\sqrt{10}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349899} {:answer/id #uuid "333c5d08-39ef-588b-b4ef-43ec1f72574f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi \\sqrt{20}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349900} {:answer/id #uuid "a50628f3-bfc7-5537-b874-58e17897f164" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi \\sqrt{15}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349901} {:answer/id #uuid "0feaf004-b11a-50cd-9964-8018dcfedbdf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi \\sqrt{30}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349902}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0feaf004-b11a-50cd-9964-8018dcfedbdf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi \\sqrt{30}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349902} :answer-field/id #uuid "b07e3e29-84e2-5989-888d-77362949d334" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349897}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "e8a7f3ab-a8b2-5b3e-a370-aad00bc69078" :question/math-academy-id "q-110252" :question/problem "Find the surface area of the part of the parametric surface $r(u,v)=⟨u,\\,v,\\,1-2u+5v⟩$ that is subject to the constraint ${u}^{2}+{v}^{2}\\le 2.$" :question/worked-solution "For a surface $S$ with parametrization $r(u,v)$ for $(u,v)\\in D,$ the surface area of $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}‖{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}‖\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWe first compute the tangent vectors to the grid curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{u}^{′} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂u},\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂u},\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂u}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂u}(u),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂u}(v),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂u}(1-2u+5v)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,\\,0,\\,-2⟩ \\\\ {r}_{v}^{′} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂v},\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂v},\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂v}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂v}(u),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂v}(v),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂v}(1-2u+5v)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,\\,1,\\,5⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the fundamental vector product is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′} & =|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 1 & 0 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 5\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =⟨|\\begin{aligned}0 & -2 \\\\ 1 & 5\\end{aligned}|,\\,-|\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 5\\end{aligned}|,\\,|\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}|⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2,\\,-5,\\,1⟩,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}‖ & =‖⟨2,\\,-5,\\,1⟩‖ \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{(2)}^{2}+{(-5)}^{2}+(1)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4+25+1} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{30}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, notice that the region ${u}^{2}+{v}^{2}\\le 2$ corresponds to the disk\n\n$$\nD={(u,v)\\,:\\,{u}^{2}+{v}^{2}\\le 2}\n$$\n\nof radius $\\sqrt{2}.$\n\nTherefore, the surface area is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}\\,\\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}‖{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}‖\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{30}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =\\sqrt{30}{∬}_{D}\\text{d}A \\\\  & =\\sqrt{30}\\cdot \\text{Area}(D) \\\\  & =\\sqrt{30}\\cdot (\\pi \\cdot (\\sqrt{2})^{2}) \\\\  & =2\\pi \\sqrt{30}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
